	"note": "Per runtime key and e2e build cell: the cell's app builds' median over the median of the honey-free reference builds timed around them in the same window (R B R B R B R; bit 21 bref, run-build.ts and build-window.ts; the reference is the honey-free vite build plus REFERENCE_WORK=15,000 units of JS work), from the RATIO build lines of the build and prod e2e jobs of 12 CI runs at ea93db8 dispatched with build_mode=record and bench_mode=record (40992951769, 40994266318, 40995964448, 40997576594, 40998707953, 41000213648, 41001335972, 41002952919, 41004626960, 41005693193, 41006989530, 41008619241). Ceiling rule (bit 21, both baselines): per cell, fit log(ratio) (mean m, sample sd s), sigma_upper = s * sqrt((n-1)/chi2_0.10,n-1) (one-sided 90% upper bound on sigma), z = Phi^-1(1 - 0.02/G) with G = 42 cells, ceiling = max(worst x 1.10, exp(m + z*sigma_upper)); baseline the median; catches = ceiling / baseline. The summed fitted per-run false-red probability is 1.91% (budget 2%); a leave-one-out backtest (ceilings re-fitted per fold) reds 0 of 12 runs. Reference choice (H1): each record also timed a bare reference (no JS work) beside the worked one; over the 12 runs both kept the ratio's runner-class shift within 10% (by CPU family and by speed half, median per job: worked -7.4% to +8.8%, bare +2.9% to +5.9%), and the worked one's in-window ratio spread is lower (2.7-3.2% against 4.0%), so the worked one is judged and the bare diagnostic is removed. A ratio cancels runner speed, not CPU microarchitecture, so ceilings span the CPU models GitHub hands out (EPYC 7763, 9V45, 9V74, Xeon 8573C, 6973P-C seen); a same-runner A/B gate (head vs a pinned base commit) replaces these ceilings next. Supersedes the report-only file from 5f2942e (two reds on unchanged code from a reference measured once per target, 40965667478 and 40969532154). No retry: a red with honey unchanged is answered by a re-record under this rule, never a rerun.",
